Subject: [#94-10-481] Criminal & Sport & Entertainment Hero Worshipp

Prof Richard Wrangham, Harvard Zoology Dept (?), gives a lecture every
year or so about his chimp research project in Africa.  One story has
to do with a group of chimps who go out looking for lone males from
another group.  They stalk and catch the individual and kill him.
This is a common practice.  Switching primates, the Brazillian rain-
forest Yanomamo, are known for their violence.  There is some evidence
to support the idea that the number of children a man fathers is related
to the number of men, from other groups, he has killed.  Or, looking at
it from the females perspective, no self-respecting Yanomamo woman would
marry a man unless he has killed one or more men.  Switching to the
Kalahari !Kung, who have a culture that may be 50-100,000 years old, a
man who can't hunt is unlikely to get a wife.  The best hunters may have
more than one wife.  Also, a man who is a really good dancer, where a
type of healing dance is a central part of their culture, gets to "lie
down with" many women.  Perhaps the current state of affairs regarding
who is admired is simply a consequence of thousands of generations of
natural selection  for murder/self-defense, sports/hunting, and entertain-
ment/healing-dance-rituals.

