Nancie Clark writes:
>When I introduced the ideas of ectogenesis and the innovations of
>mosaic birth to this conversation, I did so with the idea of  moving
>beyond our  personal views on reproduction, which  is a very
>personal and individual choice.

When applying philosophical ideas to my personal life in the present, about
all that I can reasonably say about them is, "this works *for me*, and
here's why", or "this doesn't work *for me*, and here's why".  It is
necessary to talk about the individual situation, the personal view, and
offer a local data-point; else I am second-guessing or prescribing the
behavior of others.

>Mosaic birth is not an art project to be taken lightly, it is a smart,
>intelligent way of engineering a reproduction.
"The creative process has often been compared to giving birth", you wrote
earlier. This comparison implies no lack of seriousness; participating in
this project would still require a major investment. However, genetic
engineering should allow baby-making to resemble the creative process even
more closely.

In response to my ranting:
>>Disallowing inferior genes "sets limits" on children? The blind
>>hackscrabble of Nature is superior to the caring, conscious hand of a
>>parent?

Nancy Lebovitz writes:
>At least some of the time, yes.....I can easily imagine parents
>making bad choices for their children, whether as a result of being given
>poor information by the experts or by having poor judgement
>about what their children will need.
>
True, but parental malpractice is likely to be less horrible than that of
Nature; at least parents have an interest in what's best for the child.
When such technology is available, those who won't play God will be playing
ostrich by default. "Not taking a stand is not the neutral position that it
is made out to be," to quote Roy Walford.

>...if I'd been designed to resemble my parents' favorite people,
>I would (if I still had my present temperment) *definitely* feel
>a loss of autonomy about it.

"Well, I didn't *ask* to be born, you know!" In a way, we're all
non-consensual members of families. Someone else got to choose our
existence, for their own reasons, which is certainly a poor foundation for
autonomy. When I knew that I had the freedom to exist or not, and *how* to
exist, *that* was the foundation of autonomy.  I expect that, as Mark Grant
suggested, future technologies would allow offspring who disagree with
their parent's choices to self-modify as they mature, just as many people
who disagree with Nature's choices redesign themselves today.





