I want to distinguish at least two levels of genetic design--eliminating
well-defined flaws and designing in added capabilities. (I split the
latter into three levels--adding capabilities that some people have,
adding capabilities known in other species (probably mostly animals,
but who knows?), and adding capabilities that have never been seen
before.)

I agree that eliminating well-defined flaws is an area where parents
can do better than nature, unless medical incompetence ends
up adding more or worse flaws.

However, I thought that this discussion was more about adding 
capabilities--(this may or may not have been what you meant by 
"disallowing inferior genes")--I think that if parents make major 
efforts to design their children to fill particular niches, to fit 
fashions, or to have a lot of dominance markers, the results may well 
be worse for the children than if they'd just had the luck of the
genetic draw for non-injurious traits.

I too look forward to the day when people can choose their own
genetic modifications.

